# A constant whose value is computed is built on its first use, and the
# declaration is the only place to say what it builds — so it needs a type.

fn two() -> i64 { 2 }

let VALUE = two();

fn main() {
    let x = VALUE;
}
--- errors ---
error: a top-level constant that is given something to evaluate needs a type — write `let VALUE: <type> = ...;`, since a constant computed on first use has no declaration to say what it returns
 --> ./tests/cases/constants/err_computed_value.aipl:6:13
  |
6 | let VALUE = two();
  |             ^^^^^
